@node Design
@chapter Design
@cindex design

This chapter is the design record of gate G2 (Standard §19.4): the
architecture, the concurrency decision and its trade-off (§3.2), the safety
and privacy models, the resource budgets (§22) and the choices the
requirements leave open.  The requirements say what; this chapter says how,
and may change without them.

@menu
* Architecture::        Crates and data flow.
* Concurrency::         One thread, one poll(2), and why.
* Engine design::       The pass-through tap/hold state machine.
* Sessions and ledger:: Many devices, one virtual keyboard.
* Safety model::        How the keyboard is never lost.
* Device handling::     Discovery, grab, hotplug and the I/O seam.
* Privacy model::       Where key data may go.
* Screen readers::      Orca modifier discovery.
* X11 design::          The fallback backend.
* Budgets::             Ceilings, workload and initial margins.
@end menu

@node Architecture
@section Architecture

@example
 keyboards --grab--+                          +--> uinput virtual keyboard
 pointers  --read--+--> escapepod-evdev --+   |
 inotify, signals -+                      |   |
                                          v   |
                     escapepod-core: Session (engines, ledger, chord)
                                          |
                                          +--> status socket (redacted)

 X11:  RECORD --> escapepod-x11 --> Engine --> XTEST (tap key only)
@end example

@table @code
@item escapepod-core
The engine and the multi-device session.  Pure: no I/O, no clock, no
threads, no dependencies.  Time arrives as a monotonic microsecond count, so
every decision replays in a test.
@item escapepod-evdev
Device discovery from sysfs, the exclusive grab, the uinput device and
hotplug.  The only crate that opens @file{/dev/input} or @file{/dev/uinput}.
@item escapepod-x11
The X RECORD observer and the XTEST injector.
@item escapepod
The binary: command line, configuration, diagnostics, the @code{poll(2)}
loop, signals and the status socket.
@end table

@node Concurrency
@section Concurrency
@cindex concurrency trade-off

The daemon is @strong{serial by design}.  One thread runs one
@code{poll(2)} over every device descriptor, the inotify descriptor, a
@code{signalfd} and the status socket.  The trade-off (Standard §3.2):

@itemize
@item The workload is a few events per keystroke at human rates, each
costing microseconds of engine work.  There is nothing to parallelise.
@item Modifier state is global across keyboards: a Control held on one
keyboard modifies a key on another.  Threads per device would need a shared
lock on that state and could reorder events between devices, which is a
correctness fault, not a slowdown.
@item Pass-through tapping needs no timer: the tap is decided on release.
With no timeout the daemon sleeps in @code{poll(2)} until input arrives
(ESC-SRS-057).
@end itemize

The decision is measured against the latency budget (ESC-SRS-079) and is
revisited only if that budget fails.  No async runtime is used.

@node Engine design
@section Engine design

@code{Engine::step(input, now, out)} consumes one input and appends the
outputs to a caller-owned buffer, so the hot path does not allocate once
the buffer has grown.  For a key event of key @var{k}:

@enumerate
@item If @var{k} is protected, the event passes unchanged.
@item If @var{k} is a dual-role key: a press records the press time, marks
every other held dual-role key used and emits the hold key's press at once;
an autorepeat emits the hold key's autorepeat and marks nothing; a release
emits the hold key's release and then, if the key is not used and the
release came no later than the tap timeout, one press and one release of
the tap key.
@item If @var{k} is a remap source, the same event is emitted for the
target.
@item Otherwise the event passes unchanged.
@end enumerate

Any press of a key other than the dual-role key itself, and any pointer
button press, marks every held dual-role key used.  The engine never
buffers or delays an event.  The rule ``protected beats dual-role beats
remap beats pass-through'' is checked when the rules are built, so an
ambiguous configuration is rejected rather than resolved silently.

The emergency chord is tracked over the physical keys of every device by a
separate @code{Chord} type, so a broken mapping cannot hide it.

@node Sessions and ledger
@section Sessions and ledger

Each grabbed device has its own @code{Engine}, because rules may differ
per device (ESC-SRS-034).  A @code{Session} owns the engines, the chord
tracker and the held-keys ledger, and routes events:

@itemize
@item A press on one device is also shown to every other engine as a
foreign press, so a dual-role key on a laptop is used by a key on an
external keyboard.
@item Every output passes through the @dfn{held-keys ledger}, which
counts, per key, how many devices hold it down on the virtual keyboard.
A press reaches uinput only when the count goes from 0 to 1, a release only
when it returns to 0, so two keyboards holding Control produce one Control.
@item The ledger also records which device holds which key, so removing or
faulting one device releases exactly that device's keys (ESC-SRS-052,
ESC-SRS-050), and @code{release_all} empties it on every exit path.
@end itemize

@node Safety model
@section Safety model
@cindex safety model

@enumerate
@item @strong{Output before grab.}  The uinput device is created and
announced before any keyboard is opened for a grab.  If it cannot be
created the daemon exits with code 4 and has grabbed nothing (ESC-SRS-043).
@item @strong{Grab when idle.}  Before taking a device's grab, the daemon
waits, up to 1 s, for the device to report no key down.  A key held at the
moment of the grab would otherwise have its press seen by the session and
its release lost to the grab, leaving a stuck key in the compositor.
@item @strong{Every exit releases.}  @code{SIGTERM} and @code{SIGINT}
arrive on the @code{signalfd}; the loop releases the ledger and syncs
before returning.  A panic unwinds (the release profile keeps
@code{panic = "unwind"}), and a drop guard on the virtual device releases
the ledger during the unwind.  Device removal and read errors release that
device's keys.  If the process is killed outright, the kernel drops every
grab when the descriptors close.
@item @strong{Emergency chord.}  Backspace, Escape and Enter held together
(configurable) release every key and every grab and exit with code 6,
which the service unit does not restart on.
@item @strong{Never read our own output.}  The virtual device carries a
fixed name prefix, @samp{Escape Pod virtual keyboard}, and Escape Pod's
own vendor and product identifiers; a device matching either is never
opened, whichever process created it (ESC-SRS-045).
@item @strong{Reload keeps grabs.}  A @code{SIGHUP} validates the new
configuration first.  A still-matched device keeps its descriptor and grab
and gets a fresh engine after its keys are released; only devices the new
configuration no longer matches are released.
@end enumerate

@node Device handling
@section Device handling

@strong{Discovery without device access.}  Devices are found from sysfs,
@file{/sys/class/input/event@var{N}/device/}: the name, the vendor and
product identifiers and the key capability bitmap.  This needs no read
access to any event device, which is what @code{device list} relies on
(ESC-SRS-036).  A device is a keyboard when it reports @code{KEY_A} to
@code{KEY_Z} and Enter; a pointer when it reports @code{BTN_LEFT}.

@strong{Hotplug.}  An inotify watch on @file{/dev/input} reports new
@file{event*} nodes (@code{IN_CREATE}) and permission changes made by udev
(@code{IN_ATTRIB}); a node that could not be opened at creation is retried
on its attribute change.  This replaces the @code{udev} crate named in the
PRD: it needs no C library, adds no dependency (@code{nix} already carries
inotify), and meets the 1 s hotplug requirement (ESC-SRS-051), because udev
applies the rule's permissions before announcing the device.

@strong{The I/O seam.}  @code{escapepod-evdev} exposes the operations the
loop needs (open, grab, fetch events, key state, emit, sync) behind small
traits, so the session logic is tested against fakes and the real path is
tested end to end by a uinput loopback keyboard behind the
@code{hardware-tests} feature.

@strong{Poll primitive.}  @code{nix::poll}, since @code{evdev} already
links @code{nix} 0.29; no second syscall crate enters the graph.

@node Privacy model
@section Privacy model
@cindex privacy model

@itemize
@item Only the @samp{escapepod} system account can read event devices and
write @file{/dev/uinput}; the udev rule grants that group alone, and no
login account is added to @samp{input}.
@item Key data leaves the process only through the virtual device and the
status socket (ESC-SRS-058).  Diagnostics name managed keys and devices,
never an unmanaged key code.
@item The status socket is mode @samp{0660}, group @samp{escapepod-ctl}:
reading status never grants device access.  It carries managed keys and
the engine's decisions; every other key is @samp{other}.
@item @code{event monitor --all-keys} reads devices itself and so can show
no more than its caller could already read, and it refuses to run off a
terminal or under an agent.
@end itemize

@node Screen readers
@section Screen readers
@cindex Orca

Orca keeps its modifier keys in its settings file,
@file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/orca/user-settings.conf} (JSON), under
@code{general.orcaModifierKeys} and under each profile's
@code{orcaModifierKeys}, as X keysym names such as @samp{Insert},
@samp{KP_Insert} or, for the laptop layout, @samp{Caps_Lock}.  A session
run reads that file; every named key that the configuration maps becomes
protected, with a warning naming it (ESC-SRS-038, ESC-SRS-093).  A missing
or unreadable file means no Orca modifier.  A service run reads no user's
files and protects nothing on Orca's behalf; the manual tells Orca users to
run a session daemon.  Orca versions that keep the setting only in dconf
are not read; that gap is recorded as a known limitation.

@node X11 design
@section X11 design

The X11 backend observes key and button events through a RECORD context
on a second connection and injects through XTEST.  RECORD cannot suppress
an event, so the physical key already reaches clients: the backend injects
only the tap key's press and release (ESC-SRS-088) and rejects any remap,
and any dual-role key whose hold key differs from the physical key, with
exit code 2 and a @command{setxkbmap -option} hint.  Its own injected
events come back through RECORD; the backend counts what it injected and
skips that many matching events.  X keycodes are evdev codes plus 8.

@node Budgets
@section Budgets
@cindex budgets
@cindex reference workload

Declared at G2, before the code that consumes them (§22.2).  The reference
machine is defined under @ref{Terminology}.  The @dfn{reference workload}
is 1000 taps of the dual-role key, 1000 Control chords and 10 000
pass-through keystrokes, injected through a uinput source keyboard at
20 ms spacing, with the daemon pinned by @command{taskset -c 0,1}.  The
initial margins are estimates from the design; measured margins replace
them at G3.

